  <div class="prose-legal max-w-3xl">
    <h1>About and Editorial Policy</h1>
    <p>Lawsuit Status Guide is a public legal information site for people researching active mass tort and injury lawsuits. It is not a law firm, does not provide legal advice, and does not create an attorney-client relationship.</p>

    <h2>Who prepares the site</h2>
    <p><strong>Lawsuit Status Guide Editorial Team</strong> researches, writes, and maintains the site as an organization. Pages do not claim a named attorney, doctor, or other licensed professional as an author or reviewer unless that person is identified and their role can be verified. At present, the site makes no such professional-review claim.</p>

    <h2>Primary-source hierarchy</h2>
    <p>Volatile litigation facts are checked first against primary public records. These include court orders and dockets, Judicial Panel on Multidistrict Litigation statistics, government agency materials, statutes and regulations, and official settlement-administrator notices. A manufacturer statement may document that company’s position. Secondary reporting may help locate a record, but it is not the preferred support for a current case count, deadline, ruling, hearing result, or settlement term.</p>

    <h2>How updates are reviewed</h2>
    <p>For a material update, the editorial team identifies the newest official record, confirms the event date and scope, records a direct source link, and checks the change against the prior published value. Current figures in the structured litigation panel are reconciled with the article text, timeline, FAQs, trackers, and related state pages before publication. If sources describe different scopes—for example, pending MDL actions versus administrative claims—the scopes are labeled separately rather than combined.</p>

    <h2>Neutral framing and disputed claims</h2>
    <p>The site describes allegations as allegations, identifies material defense positions when relevant, and avoids predicting eligibility, settlement value, causation, or case outcome. Settlement programs and court schedules can change, and a public case count does not measure legal merit. Medical background is informational and is not diagnosis or treatment guidance.</p>

    <h2>Dates and update labels</h2>
    <p><strong>Data through</strong> or <strong>data as of</strong> identifies the date supported by the cited source. <strong>Last updated</strong> is changed when the page receives a material factual or structural revision. <strong>Last reviewed</strong> may be changed after the page and its linked sources are checked even when no material fact changes. Scheduled events remain described as future events until an official source confirms the outcome.</p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>When the team finds a material error, it checks the underlying primary record, corrects every affected structured field and prose reference, updates the appropriate date labels, and reruns content and link checks. Readers can report a possible error through the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>. A correction request does not create an attorney-client relationship.</p>

    <h2>Editorial independence</h2>
    <p>Informational content is maintained separately from advertising disclosures and is written to remain neutral, cautious, and useful. Advertising or referral relationships do not change the source hierarchy or justify promises about a reader’s claim.</p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
